TFIIS and NMD regulate the steady-state level of AS isoforms
In plants, TFIIS contributes to the transcriptome homeostasis in several ways, including AS regulation (
Dolata et al. 2015
;
Godoy Herz et al. 2019
;
Zhu et al. 2020
;
Szadeczky-Kardoss et al. 2022
). On the other hand, NMD can detect and degrade RNA isoforms if these contain PTC and are exported to the cytoplasm for translation. To examine at what extent the TFIIS-dependent splicing generates PTC-transcripts targeted by NMD, we identified and analyzed the AS events and isoforms (AS transcript species) in our mutants and during temperature treatments. The AS events/isoforms were rare in the
nmd
single mutants. In contrast, we found a significantly increased number of AS events in
tfIIs-1
(vs Col-0) at TMHT/1d but not at TMHT/1h (
Fig. 6A
,
Supplementary Fig. S17
,
Supplementary Table S3
). This reinforces the previous finding that TFIIS is required for proper AS during persistent HS (
Szadeczky-Kardoss et al. 2022
). Notably, AS events and isoforms were synergistically enriched when
tfIIs-1
was combined with
upf1-5
and especially with
upf3-1
under all temperature conditions (
Fig. 6A
,
Supplementary Fig. S17
). These data suggest that in the absence of TFIIS, many AS transcripts are generated, and a large portion of these are stabilized in
nmd
backgrounds. This also implies that most AS products are efficiently exported and translated, therefore dangerous for proteostasis.
