TFIIS is vital under nucleotide-imbalance-induced transcriptional fidelity stress
As shown, quantitative and qualitative differences of transcriptome may cause developmental alterations and/or HS-sensitivity of TFIIS and NMD mutants (
Arciga-Reyes et al. 2006
;
Kurihara et al. 2009
;
Riehs-Kearnan et al. 2012
;
Gloggnitzer et al. 2014
;
Szadeczky-Kardoss et al. 2022
;
Obermeyer et al. 2023
). The qualitative alterations may originate from both transcriptional errors and altered AS. The importance of AS regulation during development and HSR has been extensively documented before (
Bokszczanin et al. 2013
;
Ling et al. 2018
;
Jo et al. 2022
;
Rosenkranz et al. 2022
;
Wen et al. 2023
;
Rosenkranz et al. 2024
;
Jo et al. 2025
;
Larran et al. 2025
;
Ma et al. 2025
). We assumed that reduced transcriptional fidelity could also be an important factor that strongly contributes to these phenotypes. To test whether transcriptional fidelity perturbations on its own can affect plant development and HS viability, we treated Col-0 and
tfIIs-1
plants with mycophenolic acid (MPA) or solvent control (CTL). MPA is a pharmaceutical compound that depletes intracellular guanosine triphosphate (GTP) and causes transcriptional mistakes, as shown in yeast (
Koyama et al. 2010
). (We tried to combine MPA treatment with HS but got confounding/unclear results; the effect of MPA at elevated temperatures in the plant system merits clarification.)
To test whether MPA also causes transcriptional mistakes in plants, we studied error rate changes by CirSeq (
Fig. 8
). MPA treatment led to ∼1.7-fold increased total error rates in Col-0 plants; these were significantly elevated in
tfIIs-1
mutant plants by ∼3.7-fold. Differences were found for substitutions and insertions, but not for deletions. The shifts in substitutions were generated almost exclusively by G→A changes, in correlation with the expected effect of MPA on GTP levels (
Fig. 8B
). These data de facto show that nucleotide imbalance stress affects the precision of transcription in plants.
